From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: REPLACE-ME <you@example>
Date: Tue, 06 Oct 2026 06:35:25 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] avfilter: add dovi_compose, composing a DV enhancement layer

The dovi_apply filter added earlier in this series refuses dual-layer material
outright, and it is right to: applying only the base layer of a Dolby Vision
Profile 7 stream describes a picture nobody graded. But refusing is where this
series stopped, so a stream whose residual layer carries real picture
information cannot be processed by an FFmpeg graph at all.

Clause 5.4.3.1 of ETSI GS CCM 001 says the reconstruction "adds the inverse
quantized EL signal to the MAPPED BL signal". Three things follow, and this
patch implements all three rather than only the last:

  * the residual is added in the CURVE-OUTPUT domain - after the reshaping
    curve, before the ycc_to_rgb matrix and before the PQ EOTF. Adding it after
    the matrix or after the EOTF also produces a frame that decodes and carries
    a complete set of ST 2094-40 values, so nothing downstream can tell the
    three apart; the placement is taken from the clause, not from what looks
    plausible.

  * clause 5.4.3.2's inverse quantisation is transcribed as written, including
    its `if (rr == 0) { r = 0; }` carve-out. That carve-out is load-bearing, not
    an optimisation: it is what makes a fully neutral enhancement layer add
    exactly nothing, and a port that omits it stays correct everywhere except
    that one sample.

  * the resampling filter that brings the EL onto the base layer's raster is NOT
    taken from the stream, because the stream does not carry it.
    el_spatial_resampling_filter_flag is a single bit, and clause 5.4.1 states
    that resampling "is not a normative part of the present document". The filter
    therefore requires the caller to DECLARE el_rebuild, and refuses rather than
    inventing a phase - a wrong resampling produces a picture that still
    decodes, so the only answer that cannot be missed is to stop.

dovi_compose is the filter that makes the second layer reachable from a graph.
It takes two inputs, pairs them BY PTS rather than by index (the material this
was validated on has one more base frame than enhancement frames, and the extra
one is the frame with no residual of its own), passes a base frame through and
counts it when its partner genuinely does not exist, and names every refusal it
makes. Pairing is delegated to framesync, which is what the other multi-input
video filters in-tree use; reaching activate() by hand is where the earlier
revisions of this work lost the tail frame and, twice, dereferenced a consumed
status.

Validated against the reference the kit already trusts, on a real dual-layer
subject: 49 base frames in, 49 frames out, exactly one named pass-through, the
sample-for-sample comparison against the Python reference agreeing on 98.4% of
samples bit-for-bit with the remainder explained by the resampling difference
above, and every refusal exercised through a real filtergraph.
